---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
---

<MainLayout
  title="History | The Fuzztones"
  description="The history of The Fuzztones, from the Lower East Side of New York City to decades of garage rock, touring and releases."
>
  <main>
    <header class="page-header page-header--history">
      <span class="page-header__eyebrow">The Fuzztones</span>
      <h1 class="page-title">History</h1>
      <div class="page-header__rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </header>

    <article class="history-page">
      <header class="history-page__intro">
        <h2 class="section-title">The Gurus of Garage Grunge</h2>
      </header>

      <div class="history-page__copy">
        <p>
          More than any other band, it’s The Fuzztones who are responsible for
          initiating the Garage Movement that began in the '80s and continues to
          this day. Their fiery brand of Garage-Psych Punk pre-dated the entire
          "Garage Revival" of the 1980s and has influenced hundreds of groups,
          from The Hives to The Horrors.
        </p>

        <p>
          Born in the summer of 1980 in the bowels of NYC's Lower East Side, The
          Fuzztones were soon regulars at legendary NYC hotspots such as CBGB,
          and the Mudd Club. By utilizing the fuzzbox (an antiquated effects
          pedal used by many 60s groups to achieve overly distorted
          "psychedelic" guitar sounds), the band created a raunchy sound they
          referred to as "Grunge"...
        </p>

        <p>
          On their 1984 debut single, "Bad News Travels Fast,” lead guitarist
          Elan Portnoy is credited as playing "lead grunge,” at least a decade
          before the Seattle Grunge Invasion, and the band was nicknamed "The
          Gurus of Garage Grunge."
        </p>

        <p>
          Donning paisley and leather attire, genuine human bone necklaces,
          skin-tight black jeans, black leather biker vests with the famous
          Fuzztones’ skull and crossed Vox Phantom guitar logo and Beatle boots,
          The Fuzztones influenced the fashions of musicians as diverse as the
          Hoodoo Gurus, Ian Astbury and Marc Almond, to the Dwarves.
        </p>

        <p>
          Mudhoney member Mark Arm has cited frontman Rudi Protrudi as a main
          influence. German superstars Die Artze even sport Fuzztones t-shirts
          in their promo photos, as do platinum-selling Finnish rockers, 69
          Eyes.
        </p>

        <p>
          The band's classic video for "Ward 81" even inspired the legendary
          Ramones, whose "Psychotherapy" video copies several scenes directly
          from the Fuzztones' masterpiece, as did the glitter-rock docu-film
          "Velvet Goldmine"!
        </p>

        <p>
          The band's first European tour in 1985 - a 3 month onslaught of
          England, Wales, Scotland, Germany and Italy - firmly established the
          band with a dedicated, and avid following that continues to this day!
        </p>

        <p>
          Their 1985 debut studio LP, "Lysergic Emanations" has achieved major
          cult status, and has been released by 5 different labels over the last
          16 years, and is currently enjoying it's sixth resurgence on Germany's
          Houndgawd label.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Fuzztones have toured Europe fervently since 1985, and have
          headlined major venues in Italy, Spain, Greece, France, Norway,
          Sweden, Belgium, Wales, Scotland, Austria, Germany, Czech Republic,
          Portugal, Denmark and Finland, as well as Canada, Israel, Prague,
          Serbia, Croatia and Mexico.
        </p>

        <p>
          They have appeared on over 80 vinyl and CD releases, including at
          least 15 albums, countless singles, and compilations, not to mention 5
          videos, several television and radio appearances, and countless major
          press articles (NME, Sounds, Melody Maker, Rockerilla, Hit Parade, US,
          and more) and were the only band involved in the '80s "Garage Revival"
          to obtain a major label record deal (RCA).
        </p>

        <p>
          In 2009 the band was featured on the iconic German TV show,
          Rockpalast.
        </p>

        <p>
          In 2016 England's Easy Action label released "Psychomania," a 7 cd/1
          dvd boxset as well as the entire Fuzztones back catalogue on vinyl
          (with the exception of "Horny As hell" and "Preaching To The
          Perverted").
        </p>

        <p>
          The same year saw Germany's FanPro Publishing release Fuzztones
          frontman, Rudi Protrudi's long awaited autobiography, "The Fuzztone"
          to rave reviews.
        </p>

        <p>
          Finally, 2016 was the year frontman Rudi Protrudi &amp; organist Lana
          Loveland achieved their greatest collaboration, conceiving their
          daughter, Twila May. From that point on Lana has only made infrequent
          appearances with the band, while Nico Secondini handlies much of the
          touring duties.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Fuzztones busied themselves during the 2020 lockdown by recording
          “NYC,” a tribute album to the city that originally spawned them and
          music that inspired Rudi to move there in the first place, including
          their very own versions of tunes by Patti Smith, Richard Hell, NY
          Dolls, and even Frank Sinatra’s “New York New York”!
        </p>

        <p>
          The album was followed by their 2021 release, “Encore,” as well as
          Rudi’s 360+ page photo history of the band, “As Times Gone (The
          Lysergic Legacy of The Fuzztones)” available now from FanPro.
        </p>

        <p>
          In 2023 Area Pirata released “The Fuzztones - Live at the Dive ’85,”
          and Cleopatra released “Fuzztones and Fiends,” - “A fiendishly
          eclectic collection of recent mixes and recordings from the kings of
          retro garage rock, The Fuzztones! Includes a murderer's row of
          legendary musical figures such as Screamin' Jay Hawkins, The Grass
          Roots, Ann-Margaret, Blue Cheer, Sonny Burgess and more!”
        </p>

        <p>
          In 2024, the MIG label released “the Fuzztones live at Rockpalast
          2009” CD/DVD.
        </p>

        <p>
          The same year, Punk documentarian Danny Garcia (of “Stiv,” “Looking
          For Johnny,” and “The Life and Death of Brian Jones” fame) and Chip
          Baker Films premiered the documentary, “The Fuzztones Vs The World.”
        </p>

        <p>
          Plans are currently underway for the release of the film on DVD,
          distributed by MVD Entertainment.
        </p>

        <p>
          The current band consists of Rudi Protrudi (Lead vocals, Guitar, blues
          harp), Nico Secondini (organ, vocals), Pablo Rodas (bass), Marcello
          Salis (guitar, vocals) and Marco Rivagli (drums). Although this has
          basically been the same line-up of the past 9 years, Lana Loveland
          (organ) still performs with the band occasionally.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</MainLayout>
